# Personal Rust CLI and localhost end-to-end journey

## Scope and existing evidence

Accept the smallest actual local workflow, preserve advanced theoretical tasks
separately, and label unsupported functions accurately.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Verified partial local journey — 2026-11-03

The executable Firefox/Rust integration test starts a disposable localhost
session with `--profiles-dir`, edits an exact Spanish `ñ` glyph's statistics,
adjusts both global handwriting and notebook paper values, saves both profiles
to Rust-owned local files, and reopens the selected values. The native PNG
paper preview is decoded by Firefox, and rejected invalid handwriting
statistics leave the existing file unchanged.

The independent `atrament-profiles` Rust CLI then reopens that **same saved
notebook JSON** and exports a two-page PDF plus a transparent page-two PNG at
150 DPI. The page-two browser preview and CLI PNG have identical SHA-256
hashes, proving the web and CLI paths share exact output bytes for one
admitted page. Re-exporting onto the existing PDF without `--replace` refuses
and preserves the original PDF.

**Test:** `node --test --test-name-pattern='Firefox edits, saves and reopens
both profiles through Rust localhost'
tests/browser/workspace/adapter-inbound/runtime-frame-ancestors.mjs`.
The test uses only disposable profile files, a stubbed test-owned browser
opener, real Firefox and the native Rust server and CLI.

**Updated paper-layer evidence (2026-11-03):** the same Firefox-to-Rust
journey now saves a validated numeric date with the notebook profile.
Native PDF and PNG print that numeric date using the same Rust drawing path;
invalid calendar dates refuse before output. Older notebook v1 files with
seven fields remain readable.

**Remaining:** PDF and PNG now contain physical-size paper marks, printed
numeric dates, borders and page numbers, **not handwritten text, diacritic
strokes, image overlays or general notebook content**. These are printed
annotations, not sampled pen strokes. P4-012, P5-011 and P5-012 remain open
prerequisites; production printing and the full journey are not verified.

## Saved CLI profile and print-ready Markdown journey — 2026-11-04

A repeatable real-subprocess test (`pnpm run test:print-ready`) saves one
notebook profile with a printed date, page numbers, rounded border and
preprinted-paper mode, plus a separately authored exact glyph library.
It reopens both artifacts in the same native Rust CLI and lays out Markdown
with a pastel marker-style heading scaled to 105% width and 54 explicit
hard-line rows. Both the PDF vector stream and first-page transparent PNG
are inspected for the actual pastel marker ink.
The renderer automatically paginates two physical PDF sheets and exports a
transparent RGBA page-two PNG containing the same authored colored ink.

The test passes the resulting PDF to `atrament-print --dry-run` with an
explicitly named, unconnected queue, verifying no print job is sent and
the PDF remains unchanged. A one-page forced export and unsupported glyph
both refuse without overwriting the accepted two-page document. This is
an export-only composition: text and styling are not persisted in the
notebook profile, and no browser canvas, photo layers, actual printer or
complete saved notebook document is proven here. The executable P8-009
task remains open pending the full requested CLI/localhost flow.

## Reviewed-bytes print-ready acceptance — 2026-11-04

The real native two-page Markdown journey now verifies that `atrament-print`
`--dry-run` emits the SHA-256 of the exact exported PDF as independently
calculated by Node. Uppercase `--expect-sha256` confirms the immutable PDF
snapshot without submitting a job. A separately exported, still-valid PDF
with a different heading-marker color replaces the reviewed path; requesting
physical print with the stale checksum refuses before any printer access and
leaves the altered file intact. The accepted PDF is restored and remains
unchanged through subsequent invalid marker, pagination and glyph refusals.

This uses real native Rust PDF/PNG/print CLIs but no physical printer. The
complete saved notebook content and localhost visual journey are still open.

## Browser-saved profile to decorated Markdown export (partial) — 2026-11-04

The real headless Firefox/localhost save-and-reopen journey now passes its
actual native notebook JSON, saved handwriting statistics and explicitly
browser-downloaded varied `ó` glyph strokes to one Rust CLI invocation.
The browser source field explicitly downloads an exact UTF-8 Markdown Blob;
Rust reopens that same selected `.md` file and renders its heading and body
as a single physical PDF page with an authored pastel marker band and as a
transparent RGBA PNG with the same pigment.

An unsupported authored `z` shape refuses replacement of the approved PDF.
Its exact bytes and the browser-selected glyph library remain unchanged.
The real Firefox-to-native regression passes.

The same browser-backed PDF is independently hashed with Node SHA-256, then
reviewed with the opt-in native `atrament-print --dry-run` and an explicit
unconnected queue. The Rust CLI confirms the exact reviewed digest, even in
uppercase input form, and reports no job submission. The PDF bytes remain
unchanged. This is review-only evidence, not physical printing or a workspace
print action.

This joins explicit source Markdown download with three browser-authored
profile/ink files in native decorated output. The initial source edit used
scripted DOM events rather than keyboard input; no editable notebook model
or general glyph/photo coverage is established. The complete localhost
editor journey and P8-009 acceptance remain open.

A later real-Firefox refinement exercises the Markdown source portion with
trusted WebDriver Tab, Ctrl+A, Unicode key entry and Enter. The actual
textarea bytes are asserted before download; a read-only Blob observer
checks the application's exact UTF-8 Markdown export. The already-saved
notebook and handwriting profiles and browser-authored glyph strokes then
render that selected source as real native decorated PDF and transparent
PNG, including the existing atomic missing-glyph and print-review checks.
The other profile and ink edits in this regression remain scripted rather
than an entirely keyboard-only end-to-end notebook editor journey.

## Real Firefox Unicode-space Markdown source download (partial)

A live Firefox/BiDi localhost regression now edits the authenticated source
field to each of four Unicode-only spacing glyphs: NBSP, em space, thin space
and narrow NBSP. The actual download control enables and emits an exact UTF-8
Blob for each, without inventing a font or a fallback glyph. The same test
checks BOM-prefixed NBSP and refuses both BOM-only and ASCII-only spacing.

The regression compares raw Blob octets, not just decoded `Blob.text()`,
which drops the initial UTF-8 BOM on readback even when the file preserves it.
The same exact browser-captured BOM-prefixed Markdown bytes now reopen in
an independent native Rust CLI with an explicitly authored NBSP glyph. Real
PDF and transparent RGBA PNG output match the BOM-free inline Unicode source
byte-for-byte, and the PNG contains actual selected blue ink pixels. An
unsupported em-space glyph refuses before replacing the accepted PDF.

The browser download guard now checks the original UTF-8 byte budget
before determining whether the source is empty. Even structural-only source
larger than 4,096 bytes returns an explicit size refusal without scanning
unbounded pasted text, including BOM-prefixed text and Unicode separators.

This does not supply a saved semantic notebook or in-browser PDF compilation.

## Unambiguous default native paper command (partial) — 2026-11-04

The documented `cargo run -p atrament_notebook_paper_cli -- ...` paper
examples previously failed because that package exposes two executables.
The package now declares `default-run = "atrament-paper"`, preserving
paper-only output as the implicit selection and requiring explicit
`--bin atrament-print` for any local print action.

A real Cargo subprocess regression invokes the package without `--bin`
for both PDF and PNG, then checks byte-for-byte parity with the saved
A4 grid visual candidates. It never invokes or selects a printer.
This resolves the ambiguous README command, not the broader unsaved
notebook model or localhost end-to-end acceptance.

A separate raw-stdout process check now exports both styles to PDF and PNG
without choosing any output path. Each binary stream must exactly match
the checked-in visual reference, so stray progress text or missing native
ink cannot silently corrupt shell-redirection output. The test uses
isolated operating-system temporary directories rather than requiring
an ignored repository `.temp` directory.

