## Single native paper source admission — 2026-11-10

The native notebook CLI now refuses a second `--paper-profile` flag rather
than silently applying the last fully loaded paper profile. This follows the
existing one-notebook-profile input boundary without preventing edits to a
single accepted paper profile or its notebook settings. The duplicate is
rejected before opening its selected path.

A native file-source regression checks duplicates after both a default and a
loaded notebook profile, including a nonexistent second paper path. All
refusals retain the accepted output bytes. This does not implement saved
handwritten notebook content or complete the localhost authoring journey.

## Multi-source stdin admission before reads — 2026-11-10

The native `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now rejects any second explicit
`-` stdin source among glyph libraries, physical ink and text/Markdown flow
before opening the **first** pipe. This prevents an impossible command from
waiting indefinitely for data that it could never commit. The flag scan
counts operands instead of searching free-form text, so a pasted Markdown
value or positioned text that looks like `--ink-file` is not interpreted as a
second source.

A test runs real native subprocesses with unwritten, unclosed stdin and
checks duplicate/cross-family conflicts exit, emit no output and preserve a
previously reviewed PDF. Focused Rust tests cover misleading option-like
operand values. This is command-level refusal only, not notebook content
persistence or a general source-stream multiplexer. P8-009 remains open.

## Preflight late native glyph and flow conflicts — 2026-11-10

The same flag-arity preflight also refuses a second or out-of-order glyph
library and a second text/Markdown flow source **before** reading an initial
stdin source. A later file path is never opened just to discover the input
conflict, and a caller's already reviewed PDF remains unchanged.

Rust flag-operand tests and actual unwritten-pipe subprocess regressions
cover these cases. This hardens transient CLI admission; it does not provide
a persistent notebook document or editable semantic content.

## Native profile selection before stdin consumption — 2026-11-10

Notebook CLI preflight now detects a repeated `--paper-profile` or a
repeated/late `--profile` before consuming a preceding glyph, ink, or flow
stdin pipe. The original single-profile and pre-edit ordering rules remain
unchanged. Native live-pipe regressions assert prompt refusal and unchanged
reviewed output. This is input admission, not saved notebook body support.

## Additional deferred CLI selection guards — 2026-11-10

Notebook preflight now rejects duplicate handwriting-profile, flow-region,
and output selections before a preceding pending glyph, ink, or Markdown
stdin read. Native parser and unwritten-pipe subprocess regressions confirm
no accepted output changes. Valid single selections retain their meaning;
this does not persist notebook content or complete the editor journey.

## Deferred notebook export flag preflight — 2026-11-10

The native notebook CLI now checks duplicate inspection, replace, export
format, page count, DPI, quality and PNG-page flags before reading a selected
file or an unclosed stdin pipe. Incompatible output modes, page-only options,
inspection with output, and quality/DPI conflicts refuse at the same early
boundary. Operand-looking Markdown and glyph text still remain data.

Focused native parser tests and real unwritten-pipe subprocess regressions
assert prompt refusal, empty stdout and preservation of a previously accepted
output file. This limits invalid CLI source consumption; it does not persist
semantic notebook content or complete localhost notebook editing.

## Repeated heading flag refusal before source reads — 2026-11-10

The five native heading-decoration selections now use the same early flag
admission as export options. A second style, marker/ink scale or heading/ink
color refuses before an earlier glyph, ink or Markdown stdin pipe is read.
Existing diagnostic wording is retained, and literal option-like heading
values remain operands rather than additional selections.

Focused Rust scanner tests and native unwritten-pipe subprocesses assert
prompt refusal and no replacement of reviewed output. This is bounded CLI
input safety, not automatic notebook decoration or persisted semantic flow.

## Flow and heading combinations before source reads — 2026-11-10

The native notebook CLI now validates flow/export selection, flow rectangle
requirements and Markdown-only heading customization before reading glyph,
page-ink or flow sources. Heading options use the existing typed Rust parser
and enforce their value ranges, color syntax and incompatible combinations
at that early boundary. Argument-like text operands remain literal input.

Focused Rust tests and unwritten-stdin subprocess regressions assert prompt
refusal without changing a previously reviewed PDF. This is input safety,
not persisted notebook text or a complete local editing journey.
